---
title: Keyboard shortcuts
description: Drive Pau from the keyboard and see how it shares keys with Pi.
order: 2
---

# Keyboard shortcuts

Press `⌘/` on macOS or `Ctrl+/` on Linux to see every shortcut. The list is
also under **Help > Keyboard Shortcuts**. On Linux, read `⌘` as `Ctrl` and
`⌥` as `Alt` in the table below.

| Shortcut                     | Action                              |
| ---------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| `⌘O`                         | Open a workspace                    |
| `⌘N`                         | Start a session in this workspace   |
| `Ctrl+Tab`, `Ctrl+Shift+Tab` | Next or previous session            |
| `⌘1` to `⌘9`                 | Go to the session at that position  |
| `Shift+Esc`                  | Clear attention markers             |
| `⌘L`                         | Focus the composer                  |
| `⌘[`, `⌘]`                   | Go back or forward                  |
| `⌘B`                         | Show or hide the session rail       |
| `⌥⌘B`                        | Show or hide the inspector          |
| `⌘=`, `⌘-`, `⌘0`             | Zoom in, zoom out, or reset to 100% |

The **File** menu has **New Session** and **Open Workspace**, and the **View**
menu has the zoom commands.

## Composer keys

The composer uses Pi's keys. If your `keybindings.json` assigns other keys to
one of these Pi actions, the composer uses yours instead.

| Default                  | Pi action              | Action                                     |
| ------------------------ | ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| `Enter`                  | `tui.input.submit`     | Send, or steer the agent during a run      |
| `Shift+Enter`, `Ctrl+J`  | `tui.input.newLine`    | Start a new line                           |
| `Alt+Enter`              | `app.message.followUp` | Queue a follow-up during a run             |
| `Alt+↑`                  | `app.message.dequeue`  | Move queued messages back into the composer |
| `Shift+Tab`              | `app.thinking.cycle`   | Move to the next thinking level            |
| `Ctrl+L`                 | `app.model.select`     | Open the model picker                      |
| `Esc`                    | `app.interrupt`        | Interrupt the run                          |

While a completion list is open, `Esc` closes the list and `Enter` or `Tab`
picks the highlighted item. Press `Esc` again to interrupt the run. As in Pi,
`↑` at the start of the draft and `↓` at its end browse earlier prompts.

The composer differs from Pi's editor in these ways:

- `⌘Enter` and `Ctrl+Enter` also send, unless your Pi bindings use them.
- Pau changes the thinking level and model only between runs. During a run,
  `Shift+Tab` and `Ctrl+L` explain why instead.
- When the session cannot change its thinking level, `Shift+Tab` moves focus
  to the previous control, as it does in other text fields.
- Text editing keys such as `Ctrl+A`, `Ctrl+K`, and `Alt+B` come from the
  system text field. Pi bindings for `tui.editor` actions do not change them
  or the history keys.
- Paste images with the system paste shortcut, `⌘V` on macOS.
- `Ctrl+O` shows or hides tool output and does not follow `app.tools.expand`.
- Pau has no equivalent for Pi's terminal actions: `Ctrl+C` to clear, `Ctrl+D`
  to exit, `Ctrl+Z` to suspend, and `Ctrl+G` for an external editor. It also
  has none for `Ctrl+P` to cycle models or `Ctrl+T` to fold thinking.

## Pi bindings come first

Pau reads Pi's `keybindings.json` and Pi's defaults. When a Pau shortcut uses
a key that one of your Pi bindings uses, Pi keeps the key and the Pau shortcut
stops responding to it. This mostly affects Linux, where Pi binds many `Ctrl`
keys, such as `Ctrl+N`, `Ctrl+O`, and `Ctrl+B`. The shortcut list names the Pi
action that kept each key. The menu items still work.

## Where shortcuts do not run

Shortcuts do not run while the terminal, a code editor, or a dialog has
focus, so those keys reach the program or the dialog. In the composer and
other text fields, shortcuts that editing could use, such as `⌘[` and `⌘L`,
do not run either.
